From 0f49831aa48bd16ab7ca10faa89cc648d3398c87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 23:12:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(growth): secure the growth API, add Meta ads + social +
 page metadata
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

SECURITY FIX (the important part). Every /api/growth route ran against the
SERVICE ROLE Supabase client, which bypasses RLS, and took business_id from the
request body. Any caller could therefore name any tenant: trigger their syncs,
poison their connection status, or publish a reply to their Google listing. RLS
could not save us because the service role ignores it.

  requireGrowthAccess now verifies the caller's Supabase JWT, resolves an ACTIVE
  business_membership, requires OWNER/ADMIN/MANAGER, and DERIVES business_id from
  that membership. A request naming a different tenant is rejected rather than
  silently re-scoped, so a frontend bug surfaces as a 403 instead of writing to
  the wrong tenant. /callback stays unauthenticated by necessity — the provider
  calls it — and is protected by the existing signed state.

  Verified live: no token -> 401, bogus token -> 401.

CONFIG BUG CAUGHT IN PRODUCTION. GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI is currently set to
/api/auth/google/callback, but the callback is served at /api/growth/callback.
OAuth would have failed with the user bouncing back unconnected and nothing in
the logs. setup/status now validates the path and says exactly what to change,
in both Railway and the Google Cloud OAuth client. It also returns 503 rather
than 200 when misconfigured, so the check is usable from a script.

META ADVERTISING + SOCIAL. Deliberately not modelled as "Google with different
URLs": Meta issues a short-lived token that must be exchanged for a ~60-day
long-lived one and has no refresh_token, so the callback always upgrades
immediately — storing the short token would mean sync worked exactly once.

  metaAuth.ts       OAuth dialog, code exchange, long-lived upgrade. Graph
                    version is env-overridable (META_GRAPH_VERSION, default
                    v25.0) because Meta retires versions on a ~2-year clock; a
                    hardcoded version is an outage on their schedule, not ours.
  metaProviders.ts  Ad accounts, campaigns, daily campaign insights, Facebook
                    Pages, linked Instagram accounts and media. Retries only
                    429/5xx with backoff — retrying a 4xx just burns quota.
                    Engagement rate is computed against reach, not followers,
                    because follower-based rates flatter poor distribution.
  Campaign spend rolls up into growth_channel_spend as channel "Meta", so ROAS
  and attribution keep reading one table per channel regardless of network.
  growth_ad_campaigns/growth_ad_metrics hold the per-campaign detail.

PAGE METADATA. Open Graph, Twitter Card, canonical, robots and schema.org are
now captured in the same crawl as PageSpeed and scored 0-100 for how the page
renders when shared — the most common way a boutique's link travels. A metadata
failure cannot fail the audit: a page can be slow AND unshareable, and losing
the performance numbers to a 403 helps nobody.

ACCESS REALITY, verified against Meta's permissions reference: ads_read,
instagram_basic, pages_show_list et al. require App Review AND Business
Verification for data your app does not own — but under Standard Access they
work for ad accounts and Pages held by users with a role on the app. So this
works for Roberts Enterprises immediately; App Review is only needed to onboard
other tenants. Sync errors matching Meta's opaque codes (#200/#10/#294) append
that explanation rather than leaving a bare OAuthException.

Schema 20260830000000: growth_social_accounts, growth_social_posts,
growth_social_metrics, growth_ad_campaigns, growth_ad_metrics, plus metadata
columns on growth_seo_page_results. All business_id-scoped and RLS-enforced;
verified on real Postgres 16 (applies clean, idempotent, 4 policies per table).

Frontend: publishReviewReply now sends the Supabase access token, and short
-circuits in the demo plane so the sandbox never shows an auth error to a
prospect and never reaches a real provider.

Tests 13 pass (added 6 for the metadata parser: reversed attribute order, single
quotes, entities, malformed JSON-LD beside valid, @graph nesting, and score
clamping). 15/15 guard tests, 37 frontend unit tests, typecheck clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
